* The Beginning (1943-1956)
::: columns
:::: {.column width=80%}
- @Artificial neuron@
  - Model _(McCullock-Pitts, 1943)_ based on:
    - Brain physiology
    - Propositional logic
  - Compute any function with connected neurons
    - Neuron on/off based on stimulation from neighboring neurons
    - Implement logical AND, OR, NOT with simple neuron networks

- @Alan Turing, 1947@
  - Turing test, machine learning, reinforcement learning
  - Create human-level AI by
    - Developing learning algorithms
    - Teaching machine like a child

- @Birth of AI@
  - McCarthy organized first AI workshop (1956)
  - The Logic Theorist _(Newell and Simon, 1956)_
    - Programs to "think non-numerically" and prove theorems
::::
:::: {.column width=20%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L01.3.Alan_Turing.png)

\footnotesize _Alan Turing, 1951_
::::
:::

* Enthusiasm and Great Expectations (1952-1969)
::: columns
:::: {.column width=80%}
- @Early years of AI were full of successes@
  - At the beginning, computers could only do arithmetics
  - _"A machine can never do X (e.g., games, puzzles, IQ tests, ...)"_
    - AI researchers showed machines could do one $X$ after another
  - **General Problem Solver**
    - Imitate human problem-solving
    - Consider sub-goals and possible actions

  - **Program learned to play checkers**
    - Use reinforcement learning from victories and mistakes

  - **Lisp (1958)**
    - High-level language used for 30 years in AI

- @First neural network@ _(Minsky, 1959)_
  - 3000 vacuum tubes for 40 neurons

- @MIT and Stanford@
  - Minsky at MIT: focus on neural network
  - McCarthy at Stanford: focus on representation, logic
::::
:::: {.column width=20%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L01.3.Marvin_Minsky.png)

\footnotesize _Marvin Minsky (2008)_
::::
:::

* First AI winter (1975-1980)
- @Fact@: Early successes of AI set high expectations
  - In 1965-1975 AI didn't succeed on **real problems** due to:
    - Solutions were based on _human problem-solving methods_
    - Difficulty handling _combinatorial explosion_
      - Theorem proving handles small problems with brute force, but doesn't scale
    - Neural networks couldn't be used since they needed:
      - Algorithms (e.g., backpropagation)
      - Compute power
      - Data

- @Consequence@: **First AI winter**
  - Research funding and enthusiasm dropped significantly
  - Slow AI progress through late 1970s

* Expert Systems (1980-1990)
::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Definition@: **Expert systems** (aka "knowledge-based systems")
  - Combine _weak methods_ with _extensive domain knowledge_ as rules
  - Use inference engines to apply rules to facts
  - E.g., rule-based systems, logic programming (e.g., Prolog)

- @DefinitioN@: **Weak AI** (aka "narrow AI")
  - Uses _"weak methods"_ (search, logic) that struggle to scale
  - _"Narrow AI"_ Performs specific tasks (not general reasoning) and operates in a
    limited, well-defined domain

- @Commercial adoption and industry growth@
  - AI shifted to practical applications
  - Major US corporations deployed expert systems
  - AI emerged as a commercial industry
::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

```graphviz
digraph ExpertSystem {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.4;
    ranksep=0.5;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box,
          style="rounded,filled",
          penwidth=1.8,
          fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=12,
          margin="0.22,0.14",
          height=0.50];

    edge [color="#A3B1C0",
          penwidth=1.3,
          arrowhead=vee,
          arrowsize=0.75];

    Facts  [label="Facts", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    Rules  [label="Domain rules", fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    Engine [label="Inference\nEngine", fillcolor="#FFC98A", color="#D98E2B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Conclusion [label="Conclusion", fillcolor="#9CC4F2", color="#3C6FB0", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    { rank=same; Facts; Rules; }
    Facts -> Engine;
    Rules -> Engine;
    Engine -> Conclusion;
}
```
::::
:::

* Second AI Winter (late 1980-early 1990)
- @Fact@: lots of hype / expert systems didn't deliver

- @Reasons@
  - Building/maintaining expert systems is difficult
  - Reasoning methods ignore uncertainty
  - Systems can't learn from experience
  - E.g., expert systems in medical diagnosis struggle with complex, variable
    patient data
  - E.g., early AI chess systems couldn't adapt to new strategies without manual
    updates

- @Consequence@: **Second AI winter** in late 1980-early 1990

* Return of Neural Networks (1986-)
- @Fact@: Back-propagation algorithm is (re)discovered in mid-1980s
  - Developed in early 1960s

- @Two approaches to AI are back@
  - _Connectionist paradigm_ (aka neural networks)
    - E.g., recognizing handwritten digits
  - _Symbolic paradigm_
    - E.g., solving logical puzzles with rules

- @Why connectionist approach?@
  - Concepts not well-defined using symbolic axioms
    - Form fluid internal concepts
    - Represent real-world complexity better
  - Neural networks can learn from examples
    - E.g., in image recognition identify objects by learning from labeled images

* Probabilistic Reasoning and ML (1987-)
::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @AI and scientific method@
  - Rigorous methods to test performance
    - E.g., speech recognition, handwritten character recognition
  - Benchmarks for progress, e.g.,
    - MNIST: handwritten digit recognition
    - ImageNet: image object recognition
    - SAT Competitions: boolean satisfiability solvers

- @Fact@: AI shifts from:
  - Boolean logic to probability
  - Hand-coded rules to machine learning
  - A-priori reasoning to experimental results
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Before", "After"),
  rows: (
    ("Boolean logic", "Probability"),
    ("Hand-coded rules", "Machine learning"),
    ("A-priori reasoning", "Experimental results"),
  ),
)
```
\endgroup
::::
:::

* Example: Speech Recognition
- In 1970s @ad-hoc approaches@
  - Several approaches to Rule-based systems with limited robustness
  - Cons
    - Ad-hoc, fragile
  - _"Every time I fire a linguist, the performance of the speech recognizer goes up"
    (Jelinek, 1988)_

- In 1980s @hidden Markov Models@
  - Trained on large speech corpora
  - Pros
    - Effective learning techniques
    - Strong theoretical foundation
  - HMMs became dominant

- @The bitter lesson@ _(Sutton, 2019)_
  - _"General methods + lots of data beat handcrafted systems"_

* Bayesian Networks
::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Bayesian networks@ _(Pearl, 1988)_
  - AI is linked with:
    - Probability
    - Decision theory
    - Control theory
  - Efficiently represent uncertainty
  - Provide rigorous reasoning

- @Examples@
  - Diagnosing diseases based on symptoms
  - Predictive text input in smartphones
  - Fraud detection in banking
::::
:::: {.column width=20%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L01.3.Judea_Pearl.png)

